Hey @pkutsenko,

Tried changing it to a component on this branch : https://github.com/algolia/instantsearch/commit/68c8c577592b24b1ce83166d8c4c947db290022c and I get the exact same results, so that's not the issue.

I identified another memory leak in the Node.js requester in `algoliasearch`. Some handlers that retain responses as string are not cleared. By using the fetch requester instead (install the `@algolia/requester-fetch`) and use that instead : 
```js
import { liteClient as algoliasearch } from 'algoliasearch/lite';
import { createFetchRequester } from '@algolia/requester-fetch';
const searchClient = algoliasearch(
  appId,
  apiKey,
  { requester: createFetchRequester() }
);
```

_Note that I'm using `algoliasearch` v5 with `@algolia/requester-fetch` matching the same version, should still work with v4 though but you can update if it doesn't work_

Now after 500 parallel requests it goes down to 40Mb at the end rather than 160Mb. That's using `react-instantsearch-nextjs` at `0.3.14` of course.

I still see some retained objects but I think it's Next.js/webpack at this point which we can't do anything about.
